.TH CUR-TOP 1 "2026-11-19" "cur-top 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cur-top \- list the top costs in an AWS CUR 2.0 CSV
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
Top CUR 2.0 costs from a local CSV. Reads files only, no network.

Usage: cur-top [OPTIONS] <FILE>...

Arguments:
  <FILE>...  AWS CUR 2.0 `.csv` or `.csv.gz` files

Options:
      --amount <AMOUNT>  Cost column to sum. One of the three CUR 2.0 line-item cost columns [default: line_item_unblended_cost]
      --top <TOP>        How many keys to list for each of service, usage type, and tag [default: 10]
      --json             Print JSON instead of the text report
      --md               Print a Markdown report (one table per group) instead of the text report
      --output <FILE>    Write the report to this file instead of standard output
      --quiet            Print nothing on standard error after a successful run. No context line
      --verbose          On success, print one context line on standard error
  -h, --help             Print help
  -V, --version          Print version

Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 input or usage error.
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
cur-top reads one or more local AWS Cost and Usage Report 2.0 files and lists the largest services, usage types, and resource tags of the latest billing month.
Each key shows the current amount, the amount of the previous calendar month, and the delta (current minus previous).
.PP
cur-top sums the amounts that are already in the file.
It does not multiply
.B line_item_unblended_rate
by
.BR line_item_usage_amount .
It makes no network call and uses no AWS SDK.
.PP
The input is a
.I .csv
file or a gzip
.I .csv.gz
file.
A file is read as gzip when its first bytes are the gzip magic 1f 8b, whatever its name.
A name that ends in
.I .gz
must hold gzip: without the magic the file is rejected with "not gzip".
The suffix check is case sensitive, so
.I UPPER.CSV
is rejected with "not a csv or csv.gz".
Parquet and zip are rejected.
A directory is rejected, a symlink is followed, and a file larger than 536870912 uncompressed bytes is rejected.
A gzip file is rejected when one member's compressed bytes exceed the bytes that member unpacks by more than 1048576, when the file holds more than 40000 members
("gzip overhead is larger than 1048576 bytes"),
or when the compressed bytes of every member of every input file exceed 1073741824
("gzip input is larger than 1073741824 bytes", exit 2, no report).
Unpacked bytes from an earlier member do not raise the per-member overhead or the member cap.
Measured on the release binary, one member of 20971497 unpacked bytes followed by 500000, 1000000, and 2000000 empty members of 20 bytes (10061192, 20061192, and 40061192 bytes) each exited 2 in 0.35 s (peak 2636 KB, 2688 KB, and 2896 KB).
For these empty members of 20 bytes, the measured time stayed 0.35 s when the input grew by 4 times and by 4 times again.
One CUR member, then stored members of 1048573 bytes (209711 empty stored blocks) and one shorter stored member, exited 0 at 268435456 bytes in 0.77 s (peak 2904 KB), at 536870912 bytes in 1.52 s (peak 2624 KB), and at 1073741824 bytes in 3.10 s (peak 2796 KB).
The measured time grew by 1.97 times and by 4.03 times.
A file of 1073741825 bytes exited 2 with "gzip input is larger than 1073741824 bytes" and no report in 3.12 s (peak 2848 KB).
The same row in two files is counted twice.
.PP
The current month is the latest
.B bill_billing_period_start_date
month in the files.
The previous month is the calendar month before it.
Rows of other months stay out of the totals.
.PP
Each pair in
.B resource_tags
receives the full amount of its line.
In a tag line shown in the report, a backslash in the key or in the value becomes two backslashes, and an
.B =
in the key becomes
.BR \e= .
An invalid amount, product code, usage type, billing timestamp, or tag cell is an error, and the error does not echo the cell.
ASCII space, tab, CR and LF around an amount, a billing time, a product code, a usage type, or a tag cell are removed. A no-break space, a form feed, or a vertical tab is not removed and makes that field invalid. A column name in the header must match exactly.
A monthly total that does not fit a 28-digit decimal exactly is an error ("amount total does not fit"), never a rounded amount.
The month 0000-01 is valid and has no previous month: the report shows "previous: omitted".
.PP
In the text report, a space, a control, or an invisible character in a service or usage type key is shown as
.BR \eu{..} ,
a backslash as two backslashes, and the
.B =
of
.BR amount= ,
.BR previous= ,
and
.B delta=
as
.BR \e= .
A usage error on standard error shows a control, direction-changing, or invisible character of your arguments as
.B \eu{..}
on one line, also in an argument that is not valid UTF-8.
The option
.B --json
prints the keys as they are.
The option
.B --md
prints Markdown tables with the same amounts; it cannot be combined with
.B --json
(exit 2).
In an error,
.B row N
is the record of that file, counted from 1 after the header, and every file starts again at 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --amount \fICOLUMN\fR
The cost column to sum.
One of
.BR line_item_unblended_cost ,
.BR line_item_blended_cost ,
or
.BR line_item_net_unblended_cost .
The default is
.BR line_item_unblended_cost .
The column must exist in the file.
.TP
.B --top \fIN\fR
How many keys to list for each of service, usage type, and tag.
The keys with the largest current amount come first, then by key.
.I N
runs from 1 to 10000.
The default is 10.
Totals include every row of the two months, not only the listed keys.
.TP
.B --json
Print one JSON document instead of the text report.
The first key is
.B schema_version
(now 1); it changes when a field is removed, renamed, or changes meaning.
A failing run prints no JSON.
.TP
.B --md
Print the report as Markdown tables, with the same amounts as the text report, instead of the text report.
Not allowed together with
.BR --json :
that is a usage error, exit 2.
.TP
.B --output \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to this file instead of standard output; standard output stays empty.
The report goes to a new file in the same directory and then replaces \fIFILE\fR by a rename, so \fIFILE\fR holds the whole report or is not changed.
An existing file is replaced.
A symbolic link at \fIFILE\fR is replaced as a link and never written through.
.PP
An existing pipe, socket, or device at \fIFILE\fR is refused with "not a regular file" (exit 2) and is left as it was.
A missing directory is not created.
A \fIFILE\fR that is also an input file is refused.
If the report cannot be written (a missing directory, a directory as \fIFILE\fR, a full disk, no permission) the exit status is 2 and the message is "could not write report to" and the path.
.TP
.B --quiet
Print nothing on standard error after a successful run, and no context line.
The report on standard output and the exit codes are the same.
Not allowed together with
.BR --verbose :
that is a usage error, exit 2.
.TP
.B --verbose
On success, print one
.B context:
line on standard error, for example
.BR "context: rows 3, service 2, usage_type 2, tag 1" ,
with the number of rows read and of keys listed in each table.
The report on standard output and the exit codes are the same, and an error prints no context line.
Not allowed together with
.BR --quiet .
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print the usage text.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was written.
.TP
.B 2
An input or usage error: an unreadable or unsupported file, an invalid cell, a missing column, or a bad option.
Also 2: the output cannot be written (closed or full standard output, also for the help and version text); the message is "could not write output".
The message goes to standard error.
.TP
.B 130
SIGINT (Ctrl-C) on Unix, while the files are read: no report, nothing on standard output, and the line "cur-top: interrupted by SIGINT" on standard error.
A SIGINT that arrives after the report is complete does not cut it.
.SH NOTES
Memory use is about 24 times the input size when almost every row has its own key (a 13 MB file with 300000 unique keys used 314 MiB).
The limit of 536870912 bytes applies to the input, not to the state kept for the keys.
.PP
A truncated gzip file, a wrong CRC, and bytes after the last gzip member all fail with "could not read file" and no report.
A CSV file that is cut off inside the last field of its last row, without a final newline, can be summed without an error when the amount is the last column: a CSV has no checksum.
.PP
A file is UTF-8 text; a leading BOM and CRLF line ends are allowed.
The billing time is a UTC time with a Z, for example 2026-11-01T00:00:00Z, with 1 to 6 fraction digits when there is a fraction.
A date alone, an offset, a lower case t or z, and 7 fraction digits are invalid.
A quoted cell followed by text, as in "A"x, is read as the value Ax.
Standard input is not read: \- is a file name, and /dev/stdin is rejected.
A file name must end in .csv or .gz in lower case.
.PP
A single cell is held in memory whole: a product code of 400 MiB was refused after using 913 MiB.
.PP
A standard output that was closed before the program started is recognized on Linux only.
That check (src/stdout_probe.rs) and the SIGINT handler (src/interrupt.rs) hold the only unsafe code of the project; the library forbids unsafe code.
.PP
A standard output that is open for reading only (1<file) is not recognized: on Linux the run exits 0 and writes nothing.
.PP
The Linux release binary built with glibc needs GLIBC_2.34 or newer.
A musl build works.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cur-top examples/cur.csv
cur-top --json --top 5 export-00001.csv.gz
cur-top --amount line_item_net_unblended_cost jan.csv feb.csv
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
README.md and QUALITY.md in the source tree.
